% --- Abstract ---
\begin{abstract}
Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming algorithms face a critical trade-off between maximizing visual quality and minimizing playback instability (rebuffering and bitrate switching). Traditional control-theoretic methods (e.g., RobustMPC) often suffer from severe bitrate oscillation, while existing Reinforcement Learning (RL) approaches (e.g., Pensieve) can be overly conservative or slow to react to sudden network drops due to a lack of immediate future awareness.
In this paper, we propose a \textbf{Future-Aware PPO (Proximal Policy Optimization)} agent that integrates \textit{Next-Chunk Size} prediction and \textit{Content Characteristics} (SI/TI) directly into the state space. By allowing the agent to "look ahead" just one step, we bridge the gap between the foresight of MPC and the adaptability of RL.
Extensive experiments on real-world network traces reveal that our proposed method achieves a \textbf{Quality of Experience (QoE) of 3253}, outperforming the state-of-the-art RL baseline (Pensieve) by \textbf{2.1\%}. More importantly, our method reduces bitrate switching frequency by approximately \textbf{70\%} compared to RobustMPC (5.4 switches vs. 18.2 switches), demonstrating a superior balance between high visual fidelity and playback stability.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
Adaptive Bitrate Streaming, Deep Reinforcement Learning, PPO, Future Awareness, Quality of Experience.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

% --- Section I: Introduction ---
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

\IEEEPARstart{T}{he} exponential growth of video streaming services has fundamentally reshaped internet traffic patterns. With the advent of 4K/8K resolutions and the proliferation of mobile streaming over heterogeneous networks (e.g., 5G, LTE, WiFi), delivering high-quality video with minimal stalling (rebuffering) has become a paramount challenge. To address this, Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming algorithms dynamically adjust the video bitrate for each chunk based on estimated network throughput and client buffer conditions.

The core objective of an ABR algorithm is to maximize the user's Quality of Experience (QoE), a metric that balances high visual quality against playback interruptions and smoothness. Traditional control-theoretic approaches, such as RobustMPC \cite{mpc} and Buffer-Based Approach (BBA) \cite{bba}, rely on fixed mathematical models to optimize bitrate decisions. While RobustMPC allows for explicit optimization over a prediction horizon, its performance is heavily dependent on the accuracy of throughput forecasts. As network conditions become increasingly stochastic, these prediction errors often lead to erratic behavior, manifesting as frequent quality switches (instability) that degrade the viewing experience.

In recent years, Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) has emerged as a promising alternative. The seminal work, Pensieve \cite{pensieve}, demonstrated that an ABR agent could learn to adapt to network variations purely through experience, outperforming fixed-rule algorithms. However, standard RL-based ABR agents suffer from a critical limitation: they are typically "blind" to the immediate characteristics of future video chunks. They treat the video as a sequence of generic blocks, unaware of whether the next chunk represents a complex action scene (requiring high bandwidth for acceptable quality) or a static scene. This lack of \textit{short-term foresight} makes standard RL agents reactive rather than proactive, often leading to suboptimal decisions—either becoming overly conservative to avoid rebuffering or failing to utilize available bandwidth efficiently.

In this paper, we propose a novel \textbf{Future-Aware PPO} agent that bridges the gap between the look-ahead planning of MPC and the adaptability of RL. We argue that exposing the agent to the \textit{sizes of the immediate next chunk} creates a powerful inductive bias, enabling the agent to anticipate buffer drains before they occur. Unlike prior works that use A3C, we employ Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), which offers improved sample efficiency and training stability.

Our contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Future-Aware State Space:} We design an augmented observation space that includes the precise byte-sizes of the next video chunk across all bitrate levels. This creates a "safety shield" effect, allowing the agent to identify risky high-bitrate decisions in volatile network conditions (e.g., \textit{CrowdRun} scenarios) without complex long-term forecasting.
    \item \textbf{Stability-Quality Balance:} Extensive evaluation on real-world network traces demonstrates that our method achieves a QoE of \textbf{3253}, outperforming the state-of-the-art Pensieve baseline (3185) by approximately \textbf{2.1\%}. More significantly, our approach reduces bitrate switching frequency by \textbf{$\sim$70\%} compared to RobustMPC (5.4 switches vs. 18.2 switches), solving the notorious instability problem of control-theoretic methods.
    \item \textbf{Robustness Analysis:} We provide a comprehensive ablation study and behavioral analysis, showing that our agent learns a sophisticated risk-averse policy—prioritizing stable mid-range bitrates during network volatility while aggressively capitalizing on bandwidth during stable periods.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of this paper is organized as follows: Section \ref{sec:related} reviews related work. Section \ref{sec:system} details the system model and PPO formulation. Section \ref{sec:evaluation} presents the experimental results, and Section \ref{sec:conclusion} concludes the paper.

% --- Section II: Related Work ---
\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

The optimization of Adaptive Bitrate (ABR) streaming has evolved from simple heuristics to sophisticated learning-based control systems. In this section, we review the three primary categories of existing solutions: heuristic/control-theoretic methods, reinforcement learning approaches, and content-aware streaming strategies.

\subsection{Heuristic and Control-Theoretic Schemes}
Early ABR algorithms were predominantly heuristic-based. Rate-based algorithms estimated future bandwidth based on harmonic means of past throughput, selecting the highest feasible bitrate. However, these methods proved unstable in volatile networks due to estimation errors.
To mitigate instability, Buffer-Based Approaches (BBA) \cite{bba} were introduced, which prioritize buffer occupancy over throughput estimation. While BBA effectively reduces rebuffering, it tends to be overly conservative, often failing to reach high video quality even when bandwidth is sufficient.
More advanced control-theoretic methods, notably \textit{RobustMPC} \cite{mpc}, formulate bitrate selection as a Model Predictive Control (MPC) problem. RobustMPC optimizes a specific QoE objective over a finite horizon. Despite its mathematical rigor, MPC relies heavily on the accuracy of throughput predictors. In highly dynamic environments (e.g., cellular networks or dense WiFi), prediction errors accumulate, causing the algorithm to oscillate severely between bitrates (flickering), as confirmed by our stability analysis in Section \ref{sec:evaluation}.

\subsection{Learning-Based Approaches}
The application of Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) marked a paradigm shift in ABR. \textit{Pensieve} \cite{pensieve} demonstrated that generic RL agents (using A3C) could learn policies that outperform fixed-rule algorithms without explicit network modeling. Following Pensieve, researchers explored various learning paradigms, including Imitation Learning (e.g., \textit{Comyco}) to speed up training, and Supervised Learning approaches.
However, standard RL-based ABR agents face two main challenges:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Sample Efficiency and Stability:} Many prior works utilize A3C, which can suffer from high variance in gradient updates. We leverage Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), which limits the policy update step size, ensuring more monotonic and stable convergence.
    \item \textbf{Blindness to Content:} Most RL agents treat the video as a uniform stream. They observe network state but are oblivious to the \textit{size variations} of upcoming chunks. This "blindness" forces the agent to rely solely on throughput history, making it reactive rather than proactive.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Content-Aware and Future-Aware Streaming}
Recent studies have emphasized the importance of context in streaming. Some works incorporate VMAF predictions to optimize perceptual quality rather than bitrate. Others have proposed integrating auxiliary information such as eye-tracking data or 360-degree viewpoints.
However, few works have explicitly addressed the \textbf{"Stability-Quality Trade-off"} through the lens of \textit{Future Awareness}. While some methods attempt to predict long-term bandwidth (which is error-prone), our approach takes a different path: we provide the agent with deterministic knowledge of the \textit{immediate next chunk's size}. This acts as a reliable "ground truth" for the near future, allowing the agent to make risk-averse decisions that minimize switching frequency without the computational complexity of long-horizon MPC. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to demonstrate that simple 1-step look-ahead in a PPO agent can surpass the stability of robust control methods while maintaining SOTA RL quality.
